# Caddyfile.tunnel — the tunnel-mode Caddyfile. Used ONLY with deploy/docker-compose.tunnel.yml;
# direct mode keeps using ../Caddyfile unchanged.
#
# Same three-route site as ../Caddyfile (read the comment block there for WHY /auth/v1, /rest/v1 and
# /storage/v1 have to be publicly reachable — signed storage URLs are built from this origin and the
# browser fetches them directly). The difference is that in tunnel mode the site has to be served
# TWICE, because two very different callers need it:
#
#   * cloudflared, over plain HTTP on :80. It is the only thing that can reach that port — in tunnel
#     mode Caddy publishes nothing to the host at all — and it has already terminated the public TLS
#     at Cloudflare's edge with a real, publicly-trusted certificate. Terminating a second time
#     between two containers on the same bridge buys nothing.
#
#   * the app container, over HTTPS on :443. This one is not optional. Every server-side Supabase
#     call goes to NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL, which is https://APP_HOSTNAME — it must stay the public
#     origin, because that same value is baked into the signed URLs the browser fetches. The compose
#     file gives Caddy a network alias of APP_HOSTNAME, so that name resolves to Caddy on the bridge;
#     `tls internal` is what makes the https:// half of it answer. The app trusts the resulting
#     certificate via NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS (see docker-compose.tunnel.yml).
#
# Without the HTTPS half, the app would have to resolve APP_HOSTNAME publicly and every server-side
# query would leave the building, cross Cloudflare's edge and come back through the tunnel — a round
# trip per query. With it, the origin hop never leaves the bridge.

{
	# The :80 site below is the tunnel's entry point and must SERVE the app, not bounce cloudflared to
	# HTTPS. Caddy's automatic HTTPS would otherwise install a redirect on :80 for the hostname the
	# https:// block names, and cloudflared would forward a 308 to the browser for every request.
	# Certificate automation itself is untouched — `tls internal` below still issues.
	auto_https disable_redirects
}

# Written once, imported by both site blocks. Duplicating it is how the two halves drift apart.
(ndp_routes) {
	handle /auth/v1/* {
		reverse_proxy kong:8000
	}

	handle /rest/v1/* {
		reverse_proxy kong:8000
	}

	handle /storage/v1/* {
		reverse_proxy kong:8000
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy app:3000
	}
}

# For cloudflared. Deliberately `:80` — any Host — rather than `http://{$APP_HOSTNAME}`: cloudflared
# normally passes the original Host through, but an operator who sets an origin Host override in the
# Cloudflare dashboard would otherwise get a bare 404 from Caddy with nothing explaining it. Nothing
# else can reach this port; in tunnel mode it is not published to the host.
:80 {
	import ndp_routes
}

# For the app container, on the compose bridge. `tls internal` uses Caddy's own CA, whose root is
# written to /data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt — a bind mount, which is how the app
# container gets hold of it.
https://{$APP_HOSTNAME} {
	tls internal

	import ndp_routes
}
